WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.610 --> 00:00:04.334
This video is sponsored by CuriosityStream.

00:00:06.067 --> 00:00:10.500
Jeff Bezos’ high school girlfriend once said he always wanted to make a lot of money.

00:00:10.500 --> 00:00:13.528
Because it was the only way to get what he wanted.

00:00:13.528 --> 00:00:17.098
What he wanted was to get to outer space. 

00:00:17.098 --> 00:00:20.131
Alright, and touchdown of the capsule. 

00:00:20.131 --> 00:00:26.414
Bezos may be famous for founding Amazon. But he believes his most meaningful company is Blue Origin. 

00:00:26.414 --> 00:00:30.211
This is the most important work I’m doing and I have a great conviction about that.

00:00:30.211 --> 00:00:37.135
His space venture is only six years younger than the online giant. Yet it hasn’t made anywhere near the same kind of mark. 

00:00:37.135 --> 00:00:46.384
One reason it’s moving slowly is that it’s obsessed with getting things exactly right. The logo is a feather that represents the perfection of flight.

00:00:46.384 --> 00:00:50.744
Wow. Look at that stability.

00:00:50.744 --> 00:00:56.570
The New Shepard looks beautiful as it hovers before landing. The focus has been on perfecting the technique. 

00:00:56.570 --> 00:00:59.731
But is perfection at the cost of rapid progress?

00:00:59.731 --> 00:01:02.528
SpaceX is famously not afraid to fail.

00:01:03.900 --> 00:01:06.254
They even poked fun at themselves in a video.

00:01:06.834 --> 00:01:10.871
Elon Musk once said if things are not failing, you’re not innovating enough.

00:01:12.367 --> 00:01:18.760
In October, SpaceX achieved its 100th successful flight and marked the milestone with this compilation.

00:01:18.760 --> 00:01:24.831
Meanwhile, Blue Origin hasn’t gotten into orbit once despite being two years older than SpaceX.

00:01:24.831 --> 00:01:29.917
The New Shepard doesn’t go into orbit. It only flies to the edge of space before coming back down.

00:01:29.917 --> 00:01:35.368
This is meant for tourists who have a lot of pocket change. We don’t know how much a ticket will cost.

00:01:35.368 --> 00:01:42.071
As an estimate, Virgin Galactic is selling a seat on its suborbital spaceplane for $250,000.

00:01:42.071 --> 00:01:49.100
2021 is supposed to be the big year as New Glenn is expected to fly into orbit.

00:01:49.100 --> 00:01:53.986
The heavy-lift launch vehicle that’s partially reusable remains a mystery for now.

00:01:53.986 --> 00:02:02.401
When we got a sneak peek at the mission control center inside New Glenn’s factory in Cape Canaveral, Florida, we never saw what the actual rocket looks like.

00:02:02.401 --> 00:02:05.772
Bezos has run the company pretty secretly since the beginning.

00:02:05.772 --> 00:02:11.529
He quietly told a select few Amazon employees that he started up an aerospace firm in 2000.

00:02:11.529 --> 00:02:14.545
Even three years later, word still wasn’t getting out.

00:02:14.545 --> 00:02:25.352
When Bezos was involved in a helicopter crash in Texas in 2003, news articles said he was looking at property without any mention he was also scouting out a place to build a launchpad.

00:02:25.352 --> 00:02:31.698
Blue Origin keeps quiet about its plans. But it's clear that it's moving slower than SpaceX because it can.

00:02:31.698 --> 00:02:36.578
Blue Origin can afford to take its time because it has a lot of money.

00:02:36.578 --> 00:02:45.840
Bezos puts a billion dollars’ worth of Amazon stock toward his space company every year. There's no need to race after government contracts to stay alive.

00:02:45.840 --> 00:02:49.750
Some believe this financial security negatively impacts growth.

00:02:49.750 --> 00:02:56.845
SpaceX’s COO Gwynne Shotwell once said the “free money” from Amazon as she calls it can hurt motivation and drive.

00:02:56.845 --> 00:03:00.227
SpaceX doesn’t have the luxury of big financial backing.

00:03:00.227 --> 00:03:05.730
It has to speed ahead to secure contracts like sending NASA astronauts to the International Space Station

00:03:05.730 --> 00:03:10.925
because it needs a lot of money to make Musk’s vision of colonizing Mars come true.

00:03:10.925 --> 00:03:14.320
The company has been working toward specific deadlines.

00:03:14.320 --> 00:03:18.240
It’s developing a massive, fully reusable system called Starship

00:03:18.240 --> 00:03:22.072
 to try to make it to the red planet, uncrewed, in 2024.

00:03:22.700 --> 00:03:25.704
Blue Origin’s timelines are less defined.

00:03:25.704 --> 00:03:29.070
Bezos may dream of millions of people living in space colonies.

00:03:29.070 --> 00:03:32.861
This is Maui on its best day. All year long. 

00:03:32.861 --> 00:03:36.217
However, we don’t know when these would be built and who would do it.

00:03:36.217 --> 00:03:42.078
A lack of a set date to work toward could be another reason Blue Origin appears to move with less urgency.

00:03:42.078 --> 00:03:45.587
This is how Bezos describes his company's role in his lifetime.

00:03:45.587 --> 00:03:58.159
I want to build space infrastructure so that the next generations of people can use that infrastructure the same way I used FedEx and UPS to build Amazon.

00:03:58.159 --> 00:04:07.013
Just as UPS and FedEx helped Amazon make deliveries, especially during the early days, Blue Origin hopes its spacecraft can act like a delivery service,

00:04:07.013 --> 00:04:11.752
providing future entrepreneurs a foundation to make living beyond Earth possible.

00:04:11.752 --> 00:04:12.854
Let me show you something.

00:04:18.200 --> 00:04:22.502
Blue Moon can carry 6.5 metric tons of cargo to the lunar surface. 

00:04:22.502 --> 00:04:26.819
It’s capable of navigating autonomously so it doesn’t require human hands.

00:04:26.819 --> 00:04:29.418
There’s another version designed to carry people.

00:04:29.418 --> 00:04:35.144
NASA will have to choose between this lander and the vehicles being developed by Dynetics and SpaceX.

00:04:35.144 --> 00:04:39.961
One of the three will be the first to take astronauts back to the moon by 2024.

00:04:39.961 --> 00:04:44.929
The company has gone head to head with others before and recently got stung by a big loss. 

00:04:44.929 --> 00:04:49.412
The US Air Force has been looking for a new rocket to launch top-secret missions.

00:04:49.412 --> 00:04:53.166
It no longer wants to rely on United Launch Alliance’s Atlas V

00:04:53.166 --> 00:04:58.390
because it’s powered by a Russian engine and America is trying to move away from Russian technology.

00:04:58.390 --> 00:05:06.173
ULA won 60% of the contract to develop another rocket, the Vulcan Centaur, while SpaceX received the remainder. 

00:05:06.173 --> 00:05:13.219
The Pentagon had handed Blue Origin $500 million to help develop New Glenn but in the end opted not to choose the company.

00:05:13.219 --> 00:05:18.624
After getting passed over, Blue Origin’s CEO said “we are disappointed in the decision."

00:05:18.624 --> 00:05:29.376
Despite the loss, it’s sticking by its slow and careful approach to space flight embodied in its motto Gradatim Ferociter, Latin for “step by step, ferociously".

00:05:29.376 --> 00:05:33.154
Bezos even has it stitched down the side of his lucky cowboy boots.

00:05:33.154 --> 00:05:38.201
We’ll see if those steps are ferocious enough but Bezos has always followed his instincts.

00:05:38.201 --> 00:05:47.583
All of my best decisions in business and in life have been made with heart, intuition, guts.

